# Verified 3-colourings of balls, one file per model, named by the sha256 the ball record carries.
# The RECORD is the commitment (verdict + model_sha256, checked edge by edge when it was written and re-checkable by
# `fs61_census.py scan <d> --reverify`); the model file itself is bulk regenerable data -- a single 600,000-point ball
# is 600 KB raw -- and is kept out of git.  Re-create with `fs61_census.py scan <d>` after deleting the ball record.
fields/*/models/
fields/*/quotients/

# A PARKED extraction checkpoint.  `witness-<d>/steps-ball<R>.jsonl` is a `steps.jsonl` set aside so that a DIFFERENT
# ball of the same lattice can be extracted in the same directory without the frozen `extract` silently resuming
# from it -- its resume maps the checkpoint's lattice coordinates into the new ball, and balls about one centre are
# NESTED, so every old point is found and the old kept set is picked up as though it belonged to the new ball.  That
# is sound and it is not what an upward ask is for: it would skip core round 0 on the denser ball, which is the whole
# measurement.  Park, do not delete: moving the file back restores the old route exactly.  Provenance, never evidence.
witness-*/steps-ball*.jsonl
